gm-method/rendering (a placement/offset constant calibrated against a BROKEN render becomes WRONG the moment the render bug is fixed -- a delayed second-order regression): when a model had a render bug (e.g. a double-applied import scale making it tiny) and someone hand-tuned a position/offset to compensate AROUND the broken appearance, fixing the real render bug invalidates that tuned constant. Concrete: an env model's Y offset was +4.8 (tuned while the model rendered at 1/30 scale); after the scale bug was fixed the full-size model's floor sat ~5.7m BELOW the terrain, buried, so the ground+grass drew over the lower model and only disconnected wall/roof tops poked above = the user saw 'only parts visible, and they come and go' (the emergent tops getting per-cluster frustum-culled as the camera turns). DIAGNOSIS DISCIPLINE: (1) 'come and go' + 'only parts' on a large env model first reads as an over-aggressive cull -- DISPROVE that by witness before chasing it: a force-draw A/B (monkeypatch the per-cluster cull to draw every cluster's LOD0) showed 100% of tris are present, and a frustum-vs-true-geometry comparison showed 0 in-frustum clusters wrongly culled, so the cull was CORRECT and the geometry COMPLETE; (2) the decisive witness was a SCREENSHOT (hide the loading/HUD overlay first, screenshot the canvas) -- it instantly showed the model buried in terrain with only slabs emerging, which all the numeric probes had only hinted at; (3) re-derive the offset from LIVE geometry, never trust the stale constant: read the model's world bounding-box min.y vs the terrain groundHeight at its footprint, requiredY = ground - (worldMinY - currentY). Lock with a test asserting the offset stays in the seats-on-terrain band so a revert to the old constant is caught. LESSON: after fixing any render-scale/transform bug, AUDIT every hand-tuned constant that was calibrated against the old broken appearance (position, offset, LOD distance, cull radius, camera framing) -- they are now silently wrong.